          "recap_en": "Both Japan and the UK face bond market pressure from stretched fiscal budgets and rising yields, but Japan is far better positioned due to political stability and its critical role in the AI hardware supply chain, with companies like Tokyo Electron and Kioxia. Since the Iran war began, 10-year yields have jumped to 5.04% in the UK and 2.78% in Japan, yet Japan’s Topix is up 11% year-to-date versus 4% for the FTSE 100. For investors, the UK offers only a hope of muddling through, while Japan provides genuine upside potential, making it the more attractive market.",
          "recap_zh": "日本和英国正面临债券市场的压力，但日本拥有更强的防御能力。自伊朗战争爆发以来，英国10年期国债收益率从4.25%升至5.04%，日本从2.12%升至2.78%，且日本30年期国债收益率本周触及4.13%的历史新高。日本的核心优势在于政治稳定、在AI硬件供应链中的关键地位（如东京电子和铠侠），以及日经225指数年内上涨19%、跑赢纳斯达克的表现。对投资者而言，英国可能只能勉强维持财政稳定，而日本则提供了明确的上行潜力。"
        },
        {
          "id": "bofa-daily-insights-it-still-pays-to-switch-jobs-just-not-as-much",
          "sender": "BofA",
          "title": "Daily Insights: It still pays to switch jobs, just not as much",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "en": "12976844 \n1 \n \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \nDaily Insights \nIt still pays to switch jobs, just not as much \n21 May 2026   \n  \n  \nThe job switching pay premium is at the lowest point in the past seven years \nMedian after-tax wage growth for those who stayed with their employer versus those who changed employers (Q1 of every year, YoY%) \nSource: Bank of America internal data \nBANK OF AMERICA INSTITUTE \nBank of America Institute finds job switching ticked up in Q1 2026 compared to the first quarter of last year: the share of \nemployees switching jobs rose to 13.5% in Q1 2026, up from 12.9% in Q1 2025. However, the rate of switching still remains \nbelow pre-pandemic and “Great Resignation” levels.  \nAnd while it still pays to change jobs, the after-tax wage bump is fading. The pay premium for job switchers is now the lowest \nsince the pandemic. During the “Great Resignation” period in 2022, job switchers saw an 18% year-over-year (YoY) increase in \nafter-tax wages, compared to 7% for job stayers. In Q1 2026, job switchers saw a smaller 8% YoY bump in pay, while job stayers \nsaw a 5% YoY increase.  \nOur newest publication is out now: Should I stay or should I go? The pay tradeoff. \n \n \n  \n0%\n4%\n8%\n12%\n16%\n20%\n2019\n2020\n2021\n2022\n2023\n2024\n2025\n2026\nJob stayers\nJob switchers\nI N S T I T U T E\nAccessible version",
              "zh": "12976844 \n1 \n \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n每日洞察 \n跳槽仍有回报，只是不如从前 \n2026年5月21日   \n  \n  \n跳槽薪资溢价处于过去七年最低点 \n留任者与跳槽者税后工资中位数增幅（每年第一季度，同比百分比） \n来源：美国银行内部数据 \n美国银行研究所 \n美国银行研究所发现，2026年第一季度跳槽比例较去年第一季度有所上升： \n2026年第一季度跳槽员工占比升至13.5%，高于2025年第一季度的12.9%。然而，跳槽率仍 \n低于疫情前及“大辞职潮”时期的水平。  \n尽管跳槽仍有回报，但税后工资增幅正在收窄。跳槽者的薪资溢价目前处于 \n疫情以来最低水平。在2022年“大辞职潮”期间，跳槽者税后工资同比增长18%， \n而留任者增幅为7%。2026年第一季度，跳槽者税后工资同比增幅降至8%， \n留任者同比增幅为5%。  \n我们的最新报告现已发布：《去还是留？薪资的权衡》。 \n \n \n  \n0%\n4%\n8%\n12%\n16%\n20%\n2019年\n2020年\n2021年\n2022年\n2023年\n2024年\n2025年\n2026年\n留任者\n跳槽者\n研 究 所\n无障碍版本"
            },
            {
              "en": "2 \n21 May 2026 \n \nI N S T I T U T E\n \n \nMethodology  \nSelected Bank of America transaction data is used to inform the macroeconomic views expressed in this report and should be considered in the \ncontext of other economic indicators and publicly available information. In certain instances, the data may provide directional and/or predictive \nvalue. The data used is not comprehensive; it is based on aggregated and anonymized selections of Bank of America data and may reflect a \ndegree of selection bias and limitations on the data available. \nAny payments data represents aggregated spend from US Retail, Preferred, Small Business and Wealth Management clients with a deposit \naccount or credit card. Aggregated spend include total credit card, debit card, ACH, wires, bill pay, business/peer-to-peer, cash, and checks.  \nAny Small Business payments data represents aggregate spend from Small Business clients with a deposit account or a Small Business credit \ncard. Payroll payments data include channels such as ACH (automated clearing house), bill pay, checks and wire. Bank of America per Small \nBusiness client data represents activity spending from active Small Business clients with a deposit account or a Small Business credit card and \nat least one transaction in each month. Small businesses in this report include business clients within Bank of America and generally defined as \nunder $5mm in annual sales revenue.  \nUnless otherwise stated, data is not adjusted for seasonality, processing days or portfolio changes, and may be subject to periodic revisions.  \nThe differences between the total and per household card spending growth rate (if discussed) can be explained by the following reasons:  \n1. \nOverall total card spending growth is partially boosted by the growth in the number of active cardholders in our sample. This could be due to \nan increasing customer base or inactive customers using their cards more frequently. \n2. \nPer household card spending growth only looks at households that complete at least five transactions with Bank of America cards in the \nmonth. Per household spending growth isolates impacts from a changing sample size, which could be unrelated to underlying economic \nmomentum, and potential spending volatility from less active users. \n3. \nOverall total card spending includes small business card spending while per household card spending does not. \n4. \nDifferences due to using processing dates (total card spending) versus transaction date (per household card spending). \n5. \nOther differences including household formations due to young adults moving in and out of their parent’s houses during COVID. \nAny household consumer deposit data based on Bank of America internal data is derived by anonymizing and aggregating data from Bank of \nAmerica consumer deposit accounts in the US and analyzing that data at a highly aggregated level. Whenever median household savings and \nchecking balances are quoted, the data is based on a fixed cohort of households that had a consumer deposit account (checking and/or savings \naccount) for all months from January 2019 through the most current month of data shown.  \nBank of America aggregated credit/debit card spending per household includes spending from active US households only. Only consumer card \nholders making a minimum of five transactions a month are included in the dataset. Spending from corporate cards are excluded. Data \nregarding merchants who receive payments are identified and classified by the Merchant Categorization Code (MCC) defined by financial \nservices companies. The data are mapped using proprietary methods from the MCCs to the North American Industry Classification System \n(NAICS), which is also used by the Census Bureau, in order to classify spending data by subsector. Spending data may also be classified by other \nproprietary methods not using MCCs.  \nWe consider a measure of services necessity spending that includes but is not limited to childcare, rent, insurance, insurance, public \ntransportation, and tax payments. Discretionary services includes but is not limited to charitable donations, leisure travel, entertainment, and \nprofessional/consumer services. Discretionary retail includes but is not limited to general merchandise, miscellaneous, clothing, electronics, \nfurniture. It excludes categories like groceries and gasoline. Holiday spending is defined as items in which spending in the November-December \nperiod is usually at least 20% of total annual spending on the category.  Value and premium grocers are determined judgmentally on a \nproprietary analysis of merchants by equity analysts.  \nDurables spending is defined as spending on electronics, building materials, auto and furniture. Premium durables spending is based on a \nselection of retailers who are judged to sell relatively higher value products. Conversely, value durables spending is based on a selection of \nretailers who are judged to sell relatively lower value products.  \nFor analysis looking at higher value transactions (including durables), we consider a value per transaction threshold estimated with reference to \nthe top 30% of transactions by value in 2024. The share of higher value transactions is then the number of transactions above this threshold as \na percentage of total transactions over time.  \nParticipant Pulse monitors plan participants’ behavior in Bank of America clients’ employee benefits programs, which comprise more than 4 \nmillion total participants with positive account balances as of March 31, 2026. \nLower, middle and higher household income cuts in Bank of America credit and debit card spending per household, and consumer deposit \naccount data are based on quantitative estimates of each households’ income. These quantitative estimates are bucketed according to terciles, \nwith a third of households placed in each tercile periodically. The lowest tercile represents ‘lower income’, the middle tercile represents ‘middle \nincome’ and the highest tercile ‘higher income’. The income thresholds between these terciles will move over time, reflecting any number of \nfactors that impact income, including general wage inflation, changes in social security payments and individual households’ income. The \nincome and tercile in which a household is categorised are periodically re-assessed.",
              "zh": "2 \n2026年5月21日 \n \n研 究 所\n \n \n方法论  \n本报告所阐述的宏观经济观点参考了精选的美国银行交易数据，应结合其他经济指标及公开信息进行考量。在某些情况下，该数据可能具有方向性和/或预测性价值。所用数据并非全面覆盖；其基于美国银行数据的汇总及匿名化筛选结果，可能反映出一定程度的样本选择偏差及数据可用性限制。  \n任何支付数据均代表拥有存款账户或信用卡的美国零售、优选、小企业及财富管理客户的汇总支出。汇总支出包括信用卡、借记卡、ACH、电汇、账单支付、企业/个人对个人、现金及支票等全部交易。  \n任何小企业支付数据均代表拥有存款账户或小企业信用卡的小企业客户的汇总支出。薪资支付数据涵盖ACH（自动清算系统）、账单支付、支票及电汇等渠道。美国银行小企业客户数据反映的是活跃小企业客户（拥有存款账户或小企业信用卡，且每月至少发生一笔交易）的活动支出。本报告中的小企业包括美国银行内的商业客户，通常定义为年销售收入低于500万美元的企业。  \n除非另有说明，数据未针对季节性、处理天数或投资组合变化进行调整，并可能定期进行修订。  \n总卡支出与户均卡支出增长率之间的差异（如有讨论）可由以下原因解释：  \n1.  \n总卡支出增长部分受到样本中活跃持卡人数量增长的推动。这可能是由于客户基础扩大，或非活跃客户更频繁地使用其卡片所致。  \n2.  \n户均卡支出增长仅考察每月使用美国银行卡完成至少五笔交易的家庭。户均支出增长剔除了样本规模变化（可能与潜在经济动能无关）以及非活跃用户潜在支出波动的影响。  \n3.  \n总卡支出包含小企业卡支出，而户均卡支出则不包含。  \n4.  \n差异源于使用处理日期（总卡支出）与交易日期（户均卡支出）的不同。  \n5.  \n其他差异包括疫情期间年轻人搬离或返回父母住所所导致的家庭构成变化。  \n任何基于美国银行内部数据的家庭消费存款数据，均通过对美国银行消费存款账户数据进行匿名化与汇总处理，并在高度汇总层面进行分析得出。当引用家庭储蓄与支票账户余额中位数时，数据基于一个固定家庭群体，这些家庭在2019年1月至最新数据月份期间，每月均持有消费存款账户（支票和/或储蓄账户）。  \n美国银行汇总的每户家庭信用卡/借记卡支出仅包含活跃美国家庭的数据。数据集中仅纳入每月至少完成五笔交易的消费卡持卡人。公司卡支出被排除在外。关于收款商户的数据，由金融服务公司定义的商户分类代码（MCC）进行识别与分类。这些数据通过专有方法从MCC映射至北美行业分类系统（NAICS，美国人口普查局也使用该系统），以便按子行业对支出数据进行分类。支出数据也可能通过其他不使用MCC的专有方法进行分类。  \n我们采用一项服务必需品支出衡量指标，其包括但不限于儿童保育、租金、保险、公共交通及税款支付。可自由支配服务包括但不限于慈善捐赠、休闲旅行、娱乐及专业/消费者服务。可自由支配零售包括但不限于日用百货、杂项、服装、电子产品、家具。该指标排除了食品杂货和汽油等类别。假日支出定义为在11月至12月期间的支出通常至少占该品类全年总支出20%的项目。价值型与高端型食品杂货商由股票分析师根据对商户的专有分析进行判断性确定。  \n耐用品支出定义为电子产品、建筑材料、汽车及家具方面的支出。高端耐用品支出基于被判定为销售相对高价值产品的零售商选择。反之，价值型耐用品支出基于被判定为销售相对低价值产品的零售商选择。  \n对于高价值交易（包括耐用品）的分析，我们参考2024年交易价值前30%的门槛来估算每笔交易的价值阈值。高价值交易占比即为超过此阈值的交易数量随时间推移占总交易数量的百分比。  \nParticipant Pulse监测美国银行客户员工福利计划中计划参与者的行为，截至2026年3月31日，该计划涵盖超过400万拥有正账户余额的参与者。  \n美国银行信用卡和借记卡户均支出以及消费存款账户数据中的低收入、中等收入与高收入家庭划分，基于对每个家庭收入的定量估算。这些定量估算按三分位数进行分桶，定期将三分之一的家庭归入每个分位组。最低分位组代表“低收入”，中间分位组代表“中等收入”，最高分位组代表“高收入”。这些分位组之间的收入门槛会随时间推移而变化，反映影响收入的多种因素，包括总体工资通胀、社会保障支付变动以及单个家庭的收入变化。家庭所属的收入分位组会定期重新评估。"
            },
            {
              "en": "21 May 2026\n3\n \nI N S T I T U T E\n \nMajor grocery categories include sugar and sweets, juices and other non-alcoholic beverages, bakery products, processed fruits and vegetables, \nfresh fruit and vegetables, coffee and tea, fats and oils, milk, cereal and cereal products, other, cheese, and meats, poultry and fish, Other \nincludes soups, snacks, frozen and freeze-dried prepared foods, and spices, seasonings, and condiments.  \nGenerations, if discussed, are defined as follows: Gen Z, born after 1996; Younger Millennials: born between 1989-1995; Older Millennials: born \nbetween 1978-1988; Gen Xers: born between 1965-1977; Baby Boomers: 1946-1964; Traditionalists: pre-1946. \nWhen comparing job switchers and job stayers, we are looking at a chained sample, where we compare wages in a fixed sample of people with \na consistent paycheck. For each time period – defined here as a one year period from Q1 of one year to the next – the fixed sample is updated. \nJob switchers are defined as those that received a direct deposit from another company within that time period.  \nAny reference to card spending per household on gasoline includes all purchases at gasoline stations and might include purchases of non-gas \nitems. \nAdditional information about the methodology used to aggregate the data is available upon request. \n  \n \n \nContributors \nLiz Everett Krisberg \nHead of Bank of America Institute \nDavid Michael Tinsley \nSenior Economist, Bank of America Institute",
              "zh": "2026年5月21日\n3\n \n研 究 所\n \n主要食品杂货类别包括：糖与甜食、果汁及其他非酒精饮料、烘焙产品、加工果蔬、\n新鲜果蔬、咖啡与茶、油脂、牛奶、谷物及谷物制品、其他、奶酪、以及肉类、禽类和鱼类。其他\n类别包括汤品、零食、冷冻及冻干预制食品，以及香料、调味料和佐料。\n若涉及代际划分，定义如下：Z世代（1996年后出生）；千禧一代（较年轻）：1989-1995年出生；千禧一代（较年长）：\n1978-1988年出生；X世代：1965-1977年出生；婴儿潮一代：1946-1964年出生；传统一代：1946年前出生。\n在比较换工作者与留职者时，我们采用固定样本链式分析，即对固定样本中拥有持续薪资的人群进行工资对比。\n每个时间段——此处定义为从某年第一季度至次年第一季度的一年期——固定样本会进行更新。\n换工作者定义为在该时间段内收到另一家公司直接存款的人。\n任何关于每户家庭汽油类刷卡支出的引用均包含在加油站的所有消费，并可能包含非汽油\n商品的购买。\n如需获取有关数据汇总方法的更多信息，可应要求提供。\n  \n \n \n撰稿人 \nLiz Everett Krisberg \n美国银行研究所负责人 \nDavid Michael Tinsley \n美国银行研究所高级经济学家"
            },
            {
              "en": "4 \n21 May 2026 \n \nI N S T I T U T E\n \nDisclosures \nThese materials have been prepared by Bank of America Institute and are provided to you for general information purposes only. To the extent these materials reference Bank of \nAmerica data, such materials are not intended to be reflective or indicative of, and should not be relied upon as, the results of operations, financial conditions or performance of Bank \nof America. Bank of America Institute is a think tank dedicated to uncovering powerful insights that move business and society forward. Drawing on data and resources from across \nthe bank and the world, the Institute delivers important, original perspectives on the economy, sustainability and global transformation. Unless otherwise specifically stated, any \nviews or opinions expressed herein are solely those of Bank of America Institute and any individual authors listed, and are not the product of the BofA Global Research department or \nany other department of Bank of America Corporation or its affiliates and/or subsidiaries (collectively Bank of America). The views in these materials may differ from the views and \nopinions expressed by the BofA Global Research department or other departments or divisions of Bank of America. Information has been obtained from sources believed to be \nreliable, but Bank of America does not warrant its completeness or accuracy. These materials do not make any claim regarding the sustainability of any product or service. Any \ndiscussion of sustainability is limited as set out herein.  Views and estimates constitute our judgment as of the date of these materials and are subject to change without notice. The \nviews expressed herein should not be construed as individual investment advice for any particular person and are not intended as recommendations of particular securities, financial \ninstruments, strategies or banking services for a particular person. This material does not constitute an offer or an invitation by or on behalf of Bank of America to any person to buy \nor sell any security or financial instrument or engage in any banking service. Nothing in these materials constitutes investment, legal, accounting or tax advice. \nCopyright 2026 Bank of America Corporation. All rights reserved.",
              "zh": "4  \n2026年5月21日  \n\n研 究 所  \n\n免责声明  \n本材料由美国银行研究所编制，仅供您获取一般信息之用。若本材料引用美国银行数据，则该等材料无意反映或指示美国银行的经营业绩、财务状况或表现，亦不应被依赖作为此类依据。美国银行研究所是一个致力于发掘推动商业与社会前进的深刻洞察的智库。依托全行及全球范围内的数据与资源，研究所就经济、可持续发展及全球转型提供重要的原创观点。除非另有明确说明，本文所表达的任何观点或意见仅代表美国银行研究所及所列个别作者，并非美银全球研究部门或美国银行公司及其关联公司和/或子公司（合称“美国银行”）任何其他部门的产品。本材料中的观点可能与美银全球研究部门或美国银行其他部门或分部所表达的观点和意见存在差异。信息来源于被认为可靠的渠道，但美国银行不对其完整性或准确性作出保证。本材料不对任何产品或服务的可持续性作出任何声明。任何关于可持续性的讨论均以本文所述为限。观点和估计系我们截至本材料日期的判断，如有变更，恕不另行通知。本文所表达的观点不应被解释为对任何特定个人的个性化投资建议，也不旨在作为对特定个人的特定证券、金融工具、策略或银行服务的推荐。本材料不构成美国银行代表其自身向任何人发出的购买或出售任何证券或金融工具或参与任何银行服务的要约或邀请。本材料中的任何内容均不构成投资、法律、会计或税务建议。  \n\n版权所有 2026 美国银行公司。保留所有权利。"
            }
          ],
          "sections": [
            {
              "screenshot": "data/screenshots/2026-05-23/bofa-daily-insights-it-still-pays-to-switch-jobs-just-not-as-much/pdf-page-01.png",
              "zh": "12976844 \n1 \n \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n每日洞察 \n跳槽仍有回报，只是不如从前 \n2026年5月21日   \n  \n  \n跳槽薪资溢价处于过去七年最低点 \n留任者与跳槽者税后工资中位数增幅（每年第一季度，同比百分比） \n来源：美国银行内部数据 \n美国银行研究所 \n美国银行研究所发现，2026年第一季度跳槽比例较去年第一季度有所上升： \n2026年第一季度跳槽员工占比升至13.5%，高于2025年第一季度的12.9%。然而，跳槽率仍 \n低于疫情前及“大辞职潮”时期的水平。  \n尽管跳槽仍有回报，但税后工资增幅正在收窄。跳槽者的薪资溢价目前处于 \n疫情以来最低水平。在2022年“大辞职潮”期间，跳槽者税后工资同比增长18%， \n而留任者增幅为7%。2026年第一季度，跳槽者税后工资同比增幅降至8%， \n留任者同比增幅为5%。  \n我们的最新报告现已发布：《去还是留？薪资的权衡》。 \n \n \n  \n0%\n4%\n8%\n12%\n16%\n20%\n2019年\n2020年\n2021年\n2022年\n2023年\n2024年\n2025年\n2026年\n留任者\n跳槽者\n研 究 所\n无障碍版本"
