        {
          "id": "bofa-institute-insights",
          "sender": "BofA",
          "title": "Institute Insights",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "en": "2026 Summer Travel  \nand Entertainment Outlook: \nConsumer Spending and Saving Behaviors\n Vacation Mode On:  \nAmericans Catch the Travel Bug \nAmericans are ready for a getaway this summer. More Americans (77%) are planning to travel \ncompared to previous years — surpassing both 2025 (74%) and 2024 (72%). \n• This spans all income levels, with more higher-income households planning travel: \n– Higher-income households: 93% \n– Middle-income households: 86% \n– Lower-income households: 62% \nGen Z is leading the charge, with 9 in 10 (93%) planning to travel this summer – compared with \nMillennials (85%), Gen X (73%), and Baby Boomers (62%). And Gen Z’s travel approach is distinct too: \n•  Compared with other generations, Gen Z is the most likely to travel for wellness retreats (29%), \nconcerts (32%), sporting events (20%), and weddings (17%). \nHigher gas prices are having mixed impacts on Americans’ travel plans, deterring some people entirely \nand changing plans for many others. \n•  23% are planning to reduce the number of trips that they take and 10% plan to cancel their \ntrip altogether due to increased prices. \n• However, 31% reported fuel costs have no impact on their summer plans. \nSome are making compromises to travel this summer: \n• 22% are cutting accommodation and food budgets to offset fuel costs. \nOf those traveling, the most popular choice remains domestic, with 53% crossing state lines and 17% \nexploring within their home state.  \n•  Fewer Americans say they will travel within their home state this year \n(17% in 2026, vs. 25% in 2025). \nAt the same time, the appetite for international travel has increased year-over-year (YoY). Top \ndestinations include:  \n• Europe: 24% (2025: 14%; 2024: 11%) \n•  North America outside the United States (Mexico, Canada, the Caribbean, etc.): 24% (2025: \n19%; 2024: 17%) \n• Asia: 11% (2025: 9%; 2024: 4%) \nFor those not traveling, the top three reasons are: \n• Can’t afford to travel right now: 56% \n• Uncertainty about the economy and prefer to hold off on big purchases: 25% \n• Don’t like traveling: 20% \nTravel Agentic and Chasing Value \nAI has become one of America’s favorite travel agents, with more than half (55%) using it to plan their \nsummer travels. Top uses include:  \n• Comparing costs (accommodation, flights, experiences, etc.): 28% \n• Discovering new destinations or activities: 26% \n• General travel advice or tips: 26% \nIn fact, of those who’ve used AI to plan their summer travel, more than half (56%) say AI led them to book a \ndestination or experience they would’ve never considered otherwise – this rises to 78% of Gen Z and 57% \nof Millennials.   \nWhen it comes to travel approach, 55% of Americans are chasing value – looking for great experiences \nwithin their budget.  \n•  Just 4% are prioritizing premium experiences, luxury accommodation and maximum \nconvenience regardless of cost. This is true across all income groups and generations. \nWhen Americans do decide to splurge, here’s what they prioritize: \n• Food and dining: 60% \n• Activities and experiences: 54% \n• Accommodation: 41% \nTo offset vacation costs, 46% of Americans are planning to redeem financial rewards this summer (credit \ncard rewards, banking loyalty program offers, etc.).  \n•  80% of Gen Z will supplement their vacation with financial rewards, the highest compared to other \ngenerations (Millennials: 60%, Gen X: 35%, Baby Boomer: 19%). \nWhen planning for travel, Americans primarily value the following offerings and benefits from \nfinancial institutions:  \n• Credit card rewards bonuses on top of baseline rewards: 37% \n• Personalized cash back deals (extra cash back on local dining, shopping, etc.): 30% \n• Enhanced fraud monitoring while traveling: 28% \n• Free utilization of ATMs outside of their bank’s network: 28% \n• Exclusive offers for travel and premium experiences: 26% \nBudgets Might Be Tight, but Vibes Are High \n91% of Gen Z and 80% of Millennials are planning to attend the same number or more concerts, \nsporting events, and festivals in 2026 – an increase for Gen Z (up from 85% in 2025) and a slight \ndecrease for Millennials (down from 82% in 2025). \n• Conversely, 80% of Gen X plan to attend the same number of events or fewer. \n61% of Americans (88% of Gen Z) are willing to make some sort of financial trade-off in order to afford \ntraveling to a live entertainment event. Top trade-offs include: \n• Americans overall: \n– Cutting back on dining out: 26% \n– Taking on extra work or a side hustle: 18% \n– Using credit cards: 18% \n• Gen Z: \n– Selling personal belongings (clothes, electronics, collectables etc.): 27% \n– Taking on extra work or a side hustle: 27% \n– Cutting back on dining out: 25% \nMaking every trip count: 29% of Americans who travel to live events extend the trip into a full vacation \nto explore the destination – led by Millennials (40%), followed by Gen X (29%) and Gen Z (28%). \nGen Z (80%) and Millennials (57%) are the most likely to show up to a FIFA World Cup 2026™ host city \nfor the vibes alone, even without a match ticket.  \nOver one-third (34%) of Americans are planning to set sail in the next 12 months. Gen Z takes \nthe lead (57%) followed by Millennials (44%) and Gen X (25%). Top reasons Americans are choosing \na cruise include: \n• Cruises are fun: 48% \n• The all-inclusive nature provides predictable costs and simplifies budgeting: 46% \n• They offer good overall value for the price: 45% \nHere Comes the Bill: The Cost of Love? \n57% of Americans would decline a wedding invitation from a close friend or family member if costs \nexceed their budget — up from 54% in 2025. And the financial pressure doesn’t stop at the RSVP. \nNearly half (49%) feel the financial expectations around bachelor and bachelorette parties have \nsimply “gone too far.” \nSocial media is raising the bar, with 59% of people feeling it has driven up wedding costs and \nintroduced new costs, like hiring content creators to capture and share the day.  \n• Gen Z (76%) and Millennials (65%) feel it most. \nAmong those who have received a destination wedding invitation, 43% feel excited and see it as \na great excuse to travel somewhere they might not have gone otherwise.  \n• On the flip side, 14% feel frustrated, believing it’s unfair to expect guests to spend that much. \nCash fund wedding gifts are convenient, but divisive. While 41% feel it reduces pressure and eliminates  \nthe need to shop, 35% prefer the ease of a traditional registry where they can pick something within  \ntheir budget. \nSomething old beats something new for most Americans — 41% re-wear or re-style an existing \noutfit for weddings.    \n•  Gen Z bucks the trend: only 21% reach into their closet, with the rest opting to buy (39%), \nthrift (14%), rent (13%), or borrow their look (13%). \nMethodology: The Bank of America 2026 Summer Travel and Entertainment Survey was conducted online between March 26 and April 3. \nThe survey consisted of 2,004 respondents throughout the U.S. Respondents in the study were age 18+ and were representative of the  \ncomposition of the U.S. Census for age, gender, household income and Census region.  \nGenerations are defined as follows: Gen Z: born after 1995; Younger Millennials: born between 1989-1995; Older Millennials: born between 1978-\n1988; Gen Xers: born between 1965-1977; Baby Boomers: 1946-1964; Traditionalists: pre-1946.  \nLower, middle and higher household income cuts are based on quantitative estimates of annual household income before taxes. They are defined \nas follows: lower-income households: $0-$66,000; middle-income households: $66,001-$130,000; higher-income households: >$130,000. \nSelected Bank of America transaction data is used to inform the macroeconomic views expressed in this report and should be considered in the \ncontext of other economic indicators and publicly available information. In certain instances, the data may provide directional and/or predictive \nvalue. The data used is not comprehensive; it is based on aggregated and anonymized selections of Bank of America data and may reflect a \ndegree of selection bias and limitations on the data available.  \nAny spending data represents aggregated spend from US Retail, Preferred and Wealth Management clients with a credit card. \nDisclosure: These materials have been prepared by Bank of America Institute, in partnership with Consumer Products, and are provided to you \nfor general information purposes only. To the extent these materials reference Bank of America data, such materials are not intended to be re\nflective or indicative of, and should not be relied upon as, the results of operations, financial conditions or performance of Bank of America. Bank \nof America Institute is a think tank dedicated to uncovering powerful insights that move business and society forward. For additional information \nplease click \n -\nhere. \n2026 Bank of America Corporation. All rights reserved.",
              "zh": "2026年夏季旅游  \n与娱乐展望：  \n消费者支出与储蓄行为  \n度假模式开启：  \n美国人掀起旅游热潮  \n美国人今夏已准备好外出度假。计划出游的美国人比例（77%）较往年有所上升——超过2025年（74%）和2024年（72%）。  \n• 这一趋势覆盖所有收入阶层，其中高收入家庭出游意愿更强：  \n– 高收入家庭：93%  \n– 中等收入家庭：86%  \n– 低收入家庭：62%  \nZ世代引领潮流，每10人中有9人（93%）计划今夏出游——相比之下，千禧一代（85%）、X世代（73%）和婴儿潮一代（62%）比例较低。Z世代的旅游方式也独具特色：  \n• 与其他世代相比，Z世代最倾向于为健康疗养（29%）、  \n音乐会（32%）、体育赛事（20%）和婚礼（17%）而旅行。  \n油价上涨对美国人的旅行计划影响不一，既完全打消了部分人的念头，也改变了许多人的安排。  \n• 23%的人计划减少出行次数，10%的人因价格上涨  \n而完全取消旅行计划。  \n• 然而，31%的人表示燃油成本对其夏季计划没有影响。  \n部分人今夏出行做出妥协：  \n• 22%的人削减住宿和餐饮预算以抵消燃油成本。  \n在计划出游的人群中，最热门的选择仍是国内游，53%的人将跨州旅行，17%的人  \n在所在州内探索。  \n• 今年表示将在州内旅行的美国人比例有所下降  \n（2026年为17%，2025年为25%）。  \n与此同时，国际旅游需求同比上升。热门  \n目的地包括：  \n• 欧洲：24%（2025年：14%；2024年：11%）  \n• 美国以外的北美地区（墨西哥、加拿大、加勒比等）：24%（2025年：  \n19%；2024年：17%）  \n• 亚洲：11%（2025年：9%；2024年：4%）  \n对于不出游的人群，三大主要原因如下：  \n• 目前负担不起旅行费用：56%  \n• 对经济前景不确定，倾向于推迟大额消费：25%  \n• 不喜欢旅行：20%  \n旅行自主化与追求性价比  \n人工智能已成为美国人最喜爱的旅行规划助手之一，超过半数（55%）的人使用AI规划  \n夏季旅行。主要用途包括：  \n• 比较成本（住宿、航班、体验项目等）：28%  \n• 发现新目的地或活动：26%  \n• 获取通用旅行建议或贴士：26%  \n事实上，在曾使用AI规划夏季旅行的人中，超过半数（56%）表示AI引导他们预订了  \n原本绝不会考虑的目的地或体验——这一比例在Z世代中升至78%，在千禧一代中为57%。  \n在旅行方式上，55%的美国人追求性价比——在预算范围内寻找优质体验。  \n• 仅有4%的人优先考虑高端体验、豪华住宿和  \n极致便利，不计成本。这一现象在所有收入群体和世代中均成立。  \n当美国人决定挥霍时，他们优先考虑以下方面：  \n• 餐饮美食：60%  \n• 活动与体验：54%  \n• 住宿：41%  \n为抵消度假成本，46%的美国人计划今夏兑换金融奖励（信用卡  \n奖励、银行忠诚度计划优惠等）。  \n• 80%的Z世代将用金融奖励补贴假期，比例高于其他  \n世代（千禧一代：60%，X世代：35%，婴儿潮一代：19%）。  \n在规划旅行时，美国人主要看重金融机构提供的以下优惠和福利：  \n• 基础奖励之外的信用卡奖励积分：37%  \n• 个性化返现优惠（本地餐饮、购物等额外返现）：30%  \n• 旅行期间增强型欺诈监控：28%  \n• 免费使用非本行网络ATM：28%  \n• 旅行及高端体验专属优惠：26%  \n预算或许紧张，但热情依然高涨  \n91%的Z世代和80%的千禧一代计划在2026年参加与往年相同数量或更多的音乐会、  \n体育赛事和音乐节——Z世代比例上升（2025年为85%），千禧一代则略有  \n下降（2025年为82%）。  \n• 相反，80%的X世代计划参加相同数量或更少的活动。  \n61%的美国人（88%的Z世代）愿意做出某种财务取舍，以负担  \n前往现场娱乐活动的旅行费用。主要取舍方式包括：  \n• 全体美国人：  \n– 减少外出就餐：26%  \n– 从事额外工作或副业：18%  \n– 使用信用卡：18%  \n• Z世代：  \n– 出售个人物品（衣物、电子产品、收藏品等）：27%  \n– 从事额外工作或副业：27%  \n– 减少外出就餐：25%  \n让每次旅行物超所值：29%前往现场活动的美国人将行程延伸为完整假期，  \n以探索目的地——千禧一代比例最高（40%），其次是X世代（29%）和Z世代（28%）。  \nZ世代（80%）和千禧一代（57%）最有可能仅因氛围感而前往2026年国际足联世界杯™主办城市，  \n即使没有比赛门票。  \n超过三分之一（34%）的美国人计划在未来12个月内乘坐邮轮。Z世代  \n领先（57%），其次是千禧一代（44%）和X世代（25%）。美国人选择  \n邮轮的主要原因包括：  \n• 邮轮有趣：48%  \n• 一价全包模式提供可预测成本并简化预算：46%  \n• 性价比整体较高：45%  \n账单来了：爱的代价？  \n57%的美国人会因费用超出预算而拒绝密友或家人的婚礼邀请——高于2025年的54%。财务压力并不仅限于回复邀请。  \n近半数（49%）的人认为围绕单身派对的财务期望  \n已“过于夸张”。  \n社交媒体推高了标准，59%的人认为它抬高了婚礼成本并  \n引入了新开销，例如聘请内容创作者记录和分享婚礼当天。  \n• Z世代（76%）和千禧一代（65%）感受最为强烈。  \n在收到目的地婚礼邀请的人中，43%感到兴奋，视其为  \n前往原本可能不会去的地方旅行的绝佳借口。  \n• 另一方面，14%感到沮丧，认为要求宾客花费如此之多有失公平。  \n现金礼金作为婚礼礼物方便但存在分歧。41%的人认为这减轻了压力，省去了  \n购物的麻烦，而35%的人更喜欢传统礼单的便利，可以在预算内挑选  \n物品。  \n对大多数美国人而言，旧物胜过新物——41%的人会重复穿着或改造现有  \n服装参加婚礼。  \n• Z世代反其道而行：仅21%翻找衣橱，其余人选择购买（39%）、  \n二手淘货（14%）、租赁（13%）或借用（13%）。  \n方法论：美国银行2026年夏季旅游与娱乐调查于3月26日至"
            },
            {
              "en": "12974823 \n1 \n \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \nBank of America \nInstitute Insights \n17 May 2026   \n  \n  \nUnder the big top, the economy is juggling many balancing acts. This week, Bank of America Institute invites you to step right up \nand see how consumers are performing; where summer travel is heading; as well as the thrills and chills of AI’s impact on the \nlabor market. \nThe US consumer is the ringleader for economic growth: Bank of America credit and debit card data shows spending growth was \nup 4.8% year-over-year (YoY) in April, reaching the highest level in three years. And this strength holds even when stripping out \ngasoline spending. Although, in this three-ring circus, some consumers are facing a tightrope. For lower-income households, \nwages are no longer keeping pace with spending, whereas for higher income, wage growth remains above spending. This has led \nto lower‑ and middle‑income consumers easing back on discretionary “nice‑to‑have” categories like restaurants. While \nhouseholds still have some near‑term buffers – including tax refunds and savings – these too are unevenly distributed, \nunderscoring the growing gap between headline resilience and stress experienced by some households. Read publications: \nConsumer Checkpoint: April showers and The Institute Employment Report: April 2026. \nSummer travel remains a sensation this year, with the 2026 Bank of America Summer Travel Outlook finding that most travelers \nare adjusting – not canceling – their summer travel plans in response to higher gas prices. That said, airline transactions started \nto fall in April, and a \"K-shaped\" pattern has emerged this travel season. Lower-income households are much more likely to have \nno travel plans (nearly 40%), and Bank of America card data shows their travel-related spending growth is down so far in 2026. \nPopular destinations remain concentrated stateside – led by California, Florida, Texas and New York – while international \ntravelers favor spots in Europe and other North American countries. Read publication: Summer Travel 2026: Resilient, but \nuneven. \nAI is no longer a sideshow but has moved straight into the center ring of the labor market. As it becomes woven into everyday \nwork, roughly a quarter of jobs are now exposed to generative AI – especially in higher‑income economies where non‑routine, \ncognitive tasks dominate. Still, BofA Global Research argues that how this act plays out depends less on AI itself and more on \nhow well economies adapt, doing so through skill development and overall workforce quality. Rather than wiping jobs off the bill, \nAI will reshape how the acts are performed. That said, policy can help nudge firms toward responsible and inclusive AI use, so \nproductivity gains are shared, instead of leaving some workers trapped in the tiger’s cage. Read publication: Adaptability is the \nnew job security: AI and the future of work. \n  \nI N S T I T U T E\nAccessible version",
              "zh": "12974823 \n1 \n \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n美国银行 \n研究所洞察 \n2026年5月17日   \n  \n  \n在大帐篷下，经济正同时上演多项平衡术。本周，美国银行研究所诚邀您走近观看：消费者如何表现；夏季旅游走向何方；以及人工智能对劳动力市场带来的刺激与寒意。 \n美国消费者是经济增长的领班：美国银行信用卡和借记卡数据显示，4月消费支出同比增长4.8%，达到三年来最高水平。即便剔除汽油支出，这一增长势头依然强劲。不过，在这个三环马戏团中，部分消费者正走钢丝。对于低收入家庭，工资增速已赶不上支出增速；而高收入家庭的工资增长仍高于支出。这导致中低收入消费者在餐厅等非必需“锦上添花”类消费上有所收敛。尽管家庭仍有一些短期缓冲——包括退税和储蓄——但这些资源分布不均，凸显出总体韧性与部分家庭所承受压力之间日益扩大的差距。阅读报告：《消费者观察：四月阵雨》与《研究所就业报告：2026年4月》。 \n今年夏季旅游依然火热，2026年美国银行夏季旅游展望发现，大多数旅行者面对油价上涨正在调整——而非取消——夏季出行计划。尽管如此，航空交易量在4月开始下滑，本旅游季呈现出“K型”分化格局。低收入家庭更可能完全没有出行计划（近40%），美国银行信用卡数据显示，2026年迄今其旅游相关支出增长为负。热门目的地仍集中在国内——以加州、佛罗里达、得克萨斯和纽约为首——而国际游客则青睐欧洲及其他北美国家。阅读报告：《2026年夏季旅游：韧性但不均衡》。 \n人工智能已不再是配角，而是直接进入劳动力市场的中心舞台。随着AI融入日常工作，约四分之一的工作岗位现已暴露于生成式AI的影响之下——尤其是在非程序性、认知型任务占主导的高收入经济体。不过，美银全球研究认为，这场戏如何收场，更多取决于经济体如何通过技能开发和整体劳动力质量来适应，而非AI本身。AI不会将岗位从节目单上抹去，而是会重塑表演方式。话虽如此，政策可以引导企业负责任且包容地使用AI，使生产率提升得以共享，而非让部分工人被困在虎笼之中。阅读报告：《适应性是新的工作保障：AI与未来工作》。 \n  \n研 究 所\n无障碍版本"
            },
            {
              "en": "2 \n17 May 2026 \n \nI N S T I T U T E\n \n \n \n  \n \n \nContributors \nLiz Everett Krisberg \nHead of Bank of America Institute \nDavid Michael Tinsley \nSenior Economist, Bank of America Institute",
