          "title": "Daily Insights: AI substitutes tasks, not entire jobs",
          "paragraphs": [
            {
              "en": "12977285 \n1 \n \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \nDaily Insights \nAI substitutes tasks, not entire jobs \n22 May 2026   \n  \n  \nThe number of jobs in the AI augmentation category is significantly higher than the number of jobs that have automation potential \nGlobal estimates for jobs with AI augmentation and automation potential as share of total employment \nSource: ILO – CC BY 4.0 - Gmyrek, P., Berg, J., Kamiński, K., Konopczyński, F., Ładna, A., Nafradi, B., Rosłaniec, K., Troszyński, M. (2025). Generative AI and Jobs: A Refined Global Index of Occupational Exposure, \nILO Working Paper 140 (Geneva, ILO). Doi:10.54394/HETP0387; BofA Global Research \nNote: Based on microdata for 59 countries. Augmentation potential refers to the likelihood that AI will assist humans rather than replace them. Automation potential refers to the likelihood that AI can fully \nreplace a task or job. \nBANK OF AMERICA INSTITUTE \nAs AI becomes more integrated in the workplace, BofA Global Research expects tasks to be reallocated within occupations rather \nthan entire professions being displaced. Data from the International Labour Organization (ILO) supports this view, suggesting \nthat around 13% of global employment has augmentation potential, compared with just 2.3% at risk of automation. \nHistorically, breakthrough technologies have often bolted onto existing processes before reorganizing them. Electrification, for \ninstance, first powered old factory layouts and only later enabled redesign. BofA Global Research expects a similar two-stage \npath with generative AI. \nDive deeper into our publication: Adaptability is the new job security: AI and the future of work. Or, if you’re looking for a listen \nthis Memorial Day weekend, tune in to Global Research Unlocked, where BofA Global Research analysts unpack their latest AI \ninsights. \n  \n13% (427 million jobs)\n10.4% (26 million jobs)\n12.8% (156 million jobs)\n13.5% (166 million jobs)\n13.4% (79 million jobs)\n2.3% (75 million jobs)\n0.4% (1 million jobs)\n1.3% (15 million jobs)\n2.4% (29 million jobs)\n5.1% (30 million jobs)\n0\n5\n10\n15\n20\nWorld\nLow income\nLower-middle income\nUpper-middle income\nHigh income\nWorld\nLow income\nLower-middle income\nUpper-middle income\nHigh income\nAugmentation Potential\nAutomation Potential\n% of employment\nI N S T I T U T E\nAccessible version",
              "zh": "12977285 \n1 \n \n \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n每日洞察 \nAI替代的是任务，而非整个岗位 \n2026年5月22日   \n  \n  \n具备AI增强潜力的岗位数量，显著高于存在自动化风险的岗位数量 \n全球范围内，具备AI增强潜力与自动化潜力的岗位占就业总量的估算比例 \n来源：国际劳工组织 – CC BY 4.0 - Gmyrek, P., Berg, J., Kamiński, K., Konopczyński, F., Ładna, A., Nafradi, B., Rosłaniec, K., Troszyński, M. (2025). 《生成式AI与就业：职业暴露度的精细化全球指数》， \n国际劳工组织工作文件第140号（日内瓦，国际劳工组织）。Doi:10.54394/HETP0387；美银全球研究 \n注：基于59个国家的微观数据。增强潜力指AI辅助人类而非替代人类的可能性。自动化潜力指AI能够完全 \n替代某项任务或岗位的可能性。 \n美国银行研究所 \n随着AI在工作场所的日益融入，美银全球研究预计，任务将在职业内部重新分配，而非整个职业被取代。国际劳工组织（ILO）的数据支持这一观点，表明 \n全球约13%的就业具有增强潜力，而仅有2.3%面临自动化风险。 \n从历史来看，突破性技术往往先依附于现有流程，随后再对其进行重组。例如，电气化 \n最初只是为旧工厂布局提供动力，之后才促成重新设计。美银全球研究预计，生成式AI也将经历类似的 \n两阶段路径。 \n深入了解我们的报告：《适应性是新的工作保障：AI与工作的未来》。或者，如果您想在阵亡将士纪念日周末 \n收听节目，请关注《全球研究解锁》，美银全球研究分析师将在其中解读他们最新的AI \n洞见。 \n  \n13%（4.27亿个岗位）\n10.4%（2600万个岗位）\n12.8%（1.56亿个岗位）\n13.5%（1.66亿个岗位）\n13.4%（7900万个岗位）\n2.3%（7500万个岗位）\n0.4%（100万个岗位）\n1.3%（1500万个岗位）\n2.4%（2900万个岗位）\n5.1%（3000万个岗位）\n0\n5\n10\n15\n20\n全球\n低收入国家\n中低收入国家\n中高收入国家\n高收入国家\n全球\n低收入国家\n中低收入国家\n中高收入国家\n高收入国家\n增强潜力\n自动化潜力\n占就业比例（%）\nI N S T I T U T E\n无障碍版本"
